<p>A house fire can take minutes, and the recovery can take a year. Once the fire department has cleared the scene, the next person most families meet is a <strong>fire insurance adjuster</strong>: the person who inspects the damage, reads the policy, and writes the estimate that will shape the claim. Understanding who that adjuster works for, what a fire claim includes, and how to document your loss can make a hard situation more manageable.</p>

<p>This guide is for homeowners in San Marcos, Hays County, and nearby Central Texas communities. It is general information from a licensed Texas public adjuster, not legal advice, and coverage always depends on your policy and the facts.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/fire-damage-adjuster-safety-vest.webp" alt="Adjuster in a hard hat and safety vest documenting a burned room on a tablet" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>

<h2>Three kinds of fire insurance adjusters</h2>

<p>“Fire insurance adjuster” can mean three different people, and only one of them represents you.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Who hires and pays them</th><th>Who they represent</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Staff (company) adjuster</td><td>Your insurance company</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Inspects, writes the insurer’s estimate, and applies company guidelines.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independent adjuster</td><td>The insurer, often through an adjusting firm</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Common on large or catastrophe losses; reports to the insurer’s claim staff.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public adjuster</td><td>You, under a written contract</td><td>You, the policyholder</td><td>Documents the loss, prepares an estimate and inventory support, and presents the claim on your behalf.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>On larger fire losses the insurer may also send specialists: a cause-and-origin investigator, an engineer, a contents vendor, or a mitigation company. Those people work on the insurer’s file too. It is reasonable to ask each one who hired them and to request copies of any report the insurer relies on. For a broader comparison, read <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a>.</p>

<h2>What a fire claim usually includes</h2>

<p>Most homeowners policies split coverage into parts, and a serious fire can touch every one of them. The Texas Department of Insurance home insurance guide describes these coverages in plain terms.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it generally pays for</th><th>Common fire-claim issues</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dwelling (Coverage A)</td><td>The house and attached structures</td><td>Hidden heat damage to framing, wiring, and plumbing; whether to repair or rebuild; code items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other structures</td><td>Detached garages, sheds, fences</td><td>Outbuildings and fences damaged by heat or firefighting are easy to leave off</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal property (contents)</td><td>Furniture, clothing, electronics, and other belongings</td><td>Building an itemized inventory; cleaning vs. replacing smoke-damaged items; ACV vs. replacement cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional living expenses (loss of use)</td><td>Extra costs of living elsewhere while the home is repaired, such as rent and food</td><td>Policy limits and time limits; keeping receipts; how “extra” costs are calculated</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Check your declarations page for each limit and any deductibles. If your policy pays replacement cost, the insurer often pays actual cash value first and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs or replacements are completed, so keep invoices and receipts. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains how that works.</p>

<h2>Damage that fire claims commonly miss</h2>

<h3>Smoke and soot beyond the burned area</h3>
<p>Smoke moves through the whole house: into attics, wall cavities, closets, cabinets, and the HVAC system. Rooms that look fine at a glance may carry odor and fine soot residue on walls, ceilings, and contents. Fire claim estimates sometimes limit cleaning or sealing to the room of origin. Walk every room, note odor and visible residue, and photograph surfaces such as vents, light fixtures, and the insides of cabinets. Our <a href="/services/smoke-damage-claims/">smoke damage claims</a> page goes into more detail.</p>

<h3>Water from firefighting</h3>
<p>Hoses and sprinklers can put a lot of water into a house. That water can soak drywall, insulation, subfloors, cabinets, and flooring in rooms the fire never touched, and wet materials left in place can lead to mold. Water damage from putting out a covered fire is typically part of the fire claim, but it needs to be documented and dried promptly. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Heat damage you cannot see</h3>
<p>High heat can damage wiring insulation, plumbing, roof framing, and windows well beyond the scorch marks. Ask whether the insurer’s inspection included opening walls or testing systems where heat exposure was likely, and whether an electrician or other trade has evaluated them.</p>

<h3>Contents that “look fine”</h3>
<p>Electronics, mattresses, upholstered furniture, clothing, and food can be affected by smoke, heat, or water without obvious signs. Whether an item is cleaned or replaced is a common point of negotiation.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/fire-damaged-living-room-adjuster.webp" alt="Adjuster with a clipboard inspecting a smoke- and fire-damaged living room" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Smoke and soot often travel well beyond the room where a fire started.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The first two weeks after a fire: a checklist</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Make sure everyone is safe.</strong> Do not re-enter until the fire department or a qualified professional says it is safe.</li>
<li><strong>Notify your insurer and your mortgage company.</strong> Write down the claim number, the adjuster’s name, phone number, and email. Checks for structure damage are often made payable to both you and your lender.</li>
<li><strong>Secure the property.</strong> Board-up, tarping, and fencing help prevent further damage and theft. Keep every receipt; reasonable steps to protect the property are usually part of the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Get a copy of the fire report.</strong> Ask the fire department how to request the incident report; the insurer will likely want it.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything before cleanup.</strong> Every room, every wall, ceilings, floors, closets, inside cabinets, the attic if it is safe, the HVAC unit and vents, and the exterior.</li>
<li><strong>Keep damaged items.</strong> Do not throw things away until they are documented and the insurer has had the chance to inspect them. If something must be removed for safety, photograph it first and tell the insurer in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Track every extra living expense.</strong> Hotel or rental costs, extra meals, mileage, laundry, pet boarding. Save receipts and keep a simple log.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for things in writing.</strong> Request the insurer’s estimate, photos, and any reports. Confirm phone conversations with a short email.</li>
<li><strong>Do not rush to sign.</strong> Read any release, proof of loss, or settlement document carefully, and ask questions before you sign.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a printable version of these steps.</p>

