<h2>Building a contents inventory that holds up</h2>

<p>The contents inventory is often the most time-consuming part of a fire claim. Insurers generally expect an itemized list showing what each item was, where it was, roughly when it was bought, and what it would cost to replace. TDI recommends keeping a home inventory before any loss; if you did not, you can still rebuild one.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Go room by room.</strong> Start with one room and work through closets, drawers, and shelves before moving on.</li>
<li><strong>Use what you already have.</strong> Phone photos and videos from birthdays and holidays, online order histories, credit card and bank statements, warranty registrations, and store loyalty accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Record details.</strong> Brand, model, size, quantity, age, and condition. “Sofa” is weak; “three-seat leather sofa, about 6 years old” is better.</li>
<li><strong>Separate clean vs. replace.</strong> Note which items might be cleaned and which are clearly destroyed. Keep the damaged items available until the insurer says otherwise.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the limits.</strong> Many policies have special sublimits for categories such as jewelry, firearms, collectibles, or cash. Check your policy.</li>
</ul>

<p>Expect the insurer to apply depreciation to contents. If your policy pays replacement cost on personal property, ask how and when you can recover the depreciation after you replace items.</p>

<h2>Rebuilding in San Marcos: permits and code items</h2>

<p>A significant fire rebuild will usually involve the City of San Marcos Permit Center (630 E. Hopkins Street, 512-805-2630, with online permits through MGO Connect). The city enforces the 2021 International Codes and the 2020 National Electrical Code, in effect since April 1, 2023. One local amendment worth knowing for major rebuilds: the city requires foundations to be designed by a licensed engineer. Code requirements can add cost to a rebuild, for example when older wiring, framing, or smoke alarm layouts must be brought up to current standards.</p>

<p>Code requirements do not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades. That depends on your policy’s <strong>ordinance or law</strong> coverage, which is often a separate, limited coverage. Check the limit on your declarations page. Homes in Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, New Braunfels, or other cities follow those cities’ permit rules, and Hays County does not issue residential building permits in unincorporated areas. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes page</a> has more.</p>

<h2>Texas timelines for fire claims</h2>

<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 (the Prompt Payment of Claims Act) gives insurers deadlines. The Texas Department of Insurance summarizes them in its claim-steps guide.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>General deadline</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin the investigation, and request information</td><td>Within 15 days after notice of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim</td><td>Within 15 business days after receiving all requested information; the insurer may notify you it needs more time and take up to 45 more days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay after accepting</td><td>Within 5 business days after notifying you it will pay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather catastrophe extension</td><td>Deadlines can be extended by 15 days after a declared weather-related catastrophe</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A rejection must explain the reason in writing. Insurers may make additional information requests as the investigation goes on, which is one reason large fire claims can take months. Your policy may also have its own deadlines, such as for submitting a sworn proof of loss when the insurer requests one, or for completing repairs to recover depreciation. Many Texas policies set a contractual deadline to file suit. For questions about lawsuits or deadlines to sue, talk to a Texas attorney. If your claim seems stuck, read <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/burned-interior-framing-inspection.webp" alt="Adjuster holding a tablet while inspecting burned interior framing after a house fire" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Heat damage to framing and wiring is not always visible without opening walls.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>When a public adjuster may help with a fire claim</h2>

<p>Smaller fires with limited smoke damage and a cooperative insurer are often handled well by the homeowner. A public adjuster in San Marcos may be worth calling when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The fire caused major structural damage or made the home unlivable.</li>
<li>Smoke, soot, or water damage extends well beyond the room of origin and the estimate does not reflect it.</li>
<li>You are facing a large contents inventory and do not know where to start.</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate leaves out items your contractor says are required, including code items.</li>
<li>Additional living expense payments are disputed or running out.</li>
<li>The claim is delayed, partly denied, or the cause of the fire is being questioned.</li>
</ul>

<p>A San Marcos public adjuster can help organize the documentation, prepare a detailed building estimate, support the contents inventory, track deadlines, and present the claim to the insurer. Under Texas law, a public adjuster cannot also be your contractor on the claim, cannot give legal advice, and cannot keep you from talking to your insurer. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement, and you have 72 hours after signing to cancel. Nobody can guarantee a particular result. See our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> page, or read <a href="/do-i-need-a-public-adjuster/">do I need a public adjuster?</a> for help deciding.</p>

<h2>Mistakes to avoid after a fire</h2>
<ul>
<li>Discarding damaged items or starting demolition before the loss is documented.</li>
<li>Assuming smoke damage is limited to the room that burned.</li>
<li>Letting wet materials from firefighting sit without drying.</li>
<li>Losing track of receipts for hotels, meals, and other extra costs.</li>
<li>Accepting a contents valuation without seeing how depreciation was applied.</li>
<li>Signing a release or proof of loss you do not fully understand.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Moving forward</h2>

<p>A fire claim is a long process, but it is a process: document, inventory, compare estimates, ask for explanations in writing, and keep track of deadlines. Whether you handle it yourself or bring in help, you should understand what each fire insurance adjuster on your file is doing and why.</p>

